WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.219 --> 00:00:05.369
Today is the 200th birthday of the famous German chemist

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.420
Robert Bunsen, or Bunsen as he's called in English,

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:14.360
and he of course is really famous for the Bunsen Burner,

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:19.760
and we've used it on quite a lot of our videos, never really looked at it.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.260
So come and see what a Bunsen burner is.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:28.920
It's a metal plate with a small nozzle with a tiny hole and the gas comes out

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:34.409
along this pipe and up through the hole, and on top you put this metal tube.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:37.500
You can see it's just a metal tube

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:40.560
which has a slot in the bottom.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:43.420
You put another piece of tube with a matching hole,

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:45.060
and you can rotate it,

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:47.900
so you can open and close this little window.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.700
And this gets screwed onto the top.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.500
So if we turn on the gas

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:59.900
and light the Bunsen,

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:04.619
it begins with this rather nice orange flame.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:08.500
The orange color comes from unburnt carbon.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.420
It's very hot, these tiny bits of carbon,

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:15.340
rather like you get in a candle flame.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:20.000
And so the combustion of the gas, the burning of the gas,

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.020
is not really very efficient,

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.180
and some of the carbon is being wasted.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:30.840
The natural gas that comes out of here is largely methane, CH4,

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.840
and so some of it is not burning properly.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.820
It's bright yellow because the tiny

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:37.829
particles of carbon are

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.160
so hot that they're glowing bright orange.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:45.400
The exciting thing happens when you turn the piece of metal

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:47.440
and open the little window below.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:53.700
And this Bunsen burner was invented by Bunsen and his lab assistant,

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:58.680
though most people have forgotten the name of the lab assistant now.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:00.000
Brady: What is the lab assistant's name?

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:01.960
I can't remember either.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:10.300
As I turn this, we mix the air much more efficiently with the methane, and so it burns really well.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:14.400
Scientists were really excited about the behavior of this,

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:21.560
and one of the things that Bunsen is famous for is looking at the light that was emitted from flames.

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:28.319
In fact he used this burner to discover the elements rubidium and cesium

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.079
but from the light that they emitted.

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:35.879
The rubidium and cesium were in small amounts in

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:39.029
mineral water from some German spa.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.880
He discovered them using his burner.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:47.780
One of the things people use this hot flame for is for blowing glass.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:50.720
My hands shake too much for being a very good glass blower.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:57.000
But the idea is that you rotate a piece of glass in the hottest possible part of the flame,

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.180
which is the tip of this blue cone.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:06.140
And you can see the orange color of the sodium being evaporated off the glass,

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:10.420
and the glass goes all soggy, you can see here.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:12.940
And when it's really

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:14.940
hot and soft,

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:16.810
and

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:20.010
beginning to glow quite red, you can then pull the glass out

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.740
into a fine capillary tube.

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:29.520
This was the first sort of burner,

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:33.990
the first type of burner where you could really control the size of the flame.

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:36.640
And you could make it in different sizes.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:40.020
They have the top of the Bunsen burner, was sometimes made like a fishtail,

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.940
so you got a flat flame that was quite large,

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:48.300
And so it was the ability to manipulate fire and to heat things

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:51.360
precisely that made it so important.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:56.700
When I was a student, or at school, I think the Bunsen burner

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.600
was much more an icon of chemistry than the periodic table.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:05.340
My first book, textbook,

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:11.160
on inorganic chemistry that I used as a teenager never mentioned the periodic table,

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:15.359
but it had pictures of Bunsen burners, and we used them all the time.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:20.700
So when when I first started teaching chemistry, teaching the chemistry lab,

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:24.540
I was teaching their lab over there on the other side of the building,

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:26.680
and a really stupid student,

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.720
and I'm sorry to say it - he was stupid,

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:33.800
managed to put the rubber tube from his Bunsen burner

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:36.400
under the flame and

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:39.560
the tube caught fire and burnt through.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:46.100
And suddenly there was a flame coming out of the tube, like a flame thrower! All over the bench!

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:48.940
And it was at that moment

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:54.220
that I realized that I didn't know where the fire extinguisher was.

00:04:55.340 --> 00:05:00.120
And it took me nearly a minute to find the fire extinguisher,

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:03.779
but by which time the whole bench was burning!

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:09.809
And we put out the fire, but for the next few years, every time I went into that lab,

00:05:09.809 --> 00:05:15.040
I saw a black stain on the bench to show how stupid I was

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:18.500
that I was running a lab without knowing where the fire extinguisher was.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:23.340
Bunsen was a really talented chemist

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:25.180
who made contributions

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:28.440
over large areas of chemistry.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:30.440
He discovered the elements.

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:33.120
He laid the foundations for spectroscopy.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.700
I think probably he would be a little bit disappointed that

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:42.240
he's being remembered on his birthday for the Bunsen burner,

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:46.820
because I'm sure he didn't think it was the most important part of his career.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:48.480
Bunsen

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.660
was very famous as a very good glass blower.

00:05:52.680 --> 00:06:00.180
And, apparently, he could glass blow until his fingers were smoking and had gone black.

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:03.820
The nerves in his fingertips had gone.

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:06.320
I've burnt myself quite often glass blowing.

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:09.600
I never had my fingers going black or smoking.

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:12.780
And anyway, smoking isn't allowed in the lab anymore.

